module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_wall_conjunct_requires_a_class_no_producer_can_emit" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a wall carries a non-emptiness conjunct over a derived population — `length(xs: roster()) > 0`, a `count > 0`, a `both sides present` pair — whose CLASS has no producer anywhere in the tree. The class is not merely unpopulated today; nothing constructs it. The wall is therefore not red-as-in-broken and not red-as-in-awaiting-a-row: it is permanently unsatisfiable, and it will keep reading as slop debt in the ledger until someone either manufactures a specimen to satisfy it or identifies the missing PRODUCER as the actual trigger.",

    "HARM, AND IT IS NOT THE RED. The red is honest. The harm is the misfiled trigger and the two wrong repairs it invites. (1) DELETING THE CONJUNCT to go green, which deletes the discrimination too: a wall that no longer requires the class to be inhabited also stops noticing when the class is emptied by accident, so the next real regression in that classification is silent. (2) MANUFACTURING A SPECIMEN to satisfy the conjunct — a row written to have the shape the roster filter tests, which turns a claim about the compiler's behaviour into a claim about a literal, and goes green while the behaviour it was written to pin down does not exist. Both repairs look like closing the debt; both destroy the witness.",

    "MEASURED, this session, on the admission-witness trio (gunbc, origin/main 98e4b52575). Two witnesses are red for exactly this reason: test.claim.exact_witness_admission_witness.no_batch_mixes_a_pre_verdict_row_with_an_assertion_false_row fails its FINAL conjunct `length(xs: pre_verdict_sides) > 0`, and both_red_expectations_are_known_red_but_only_one_expects_a_pre_verdict_refusal fails on `pre_verdict_count > 0` over the same population. Both rosters derive from `known_red_pre_verdict_refusal_roster()`, which filters the ledger for rows declaring `ExpectTypedPreVerdictRefusal`; the ledger contains ZERO such rows, and its constructor `known_red_pre_verdict_probe` has no call sites at all.",

    "THE PRODUCER DOES NOT EXIST, AND THAT IS THE TRIGGER TO NAME. No code anywhere classifies a probe as stopping before a verdict: `expect_pre_verdict_refusal` has no definition in .dag or .rs, and `PreVerdict` / `PreVerdictResolve` / `PreVerdictCodegen` / `UnboundImportedVariantConstructor` appear in exactly three files, all of them the claim side (gunbc.std.witness_admission, gunbc.explicit_witness_admission, and this test module). The classifier was deleted 2026-10-14 by #13861 (29a76c8a8b, `Delete unreached claim_executor code`), which removed the Rust `PreVerdictPhase` enum and `PreVerdictCodegenError`; since then the vocabulary has had a consumer and no producer. So the trigger for these two witnesses is NOT a ledger edit — it is the absence of a probe host that can report a refusal cause from BEFORE the verdict phase.",

    "HOW THE EMPTY CLASS WAS PROVEN EMPTY RATHER THAN MERELY UNLISTED. All eighteen rows failing the well-formedness wall (the sixteen still quarantined plus the two constructor_tag_route_probe_red rows live at measurement time) were probed INDIVIDUALLY under claim_batch on the runner: each printed a bare `FAIL <fn>` — the claim_batch printer for a witness that RESOLVED and RETURNED FALSE. A resolve failure prints `resolve failed: <error>` instead, so none of the eighteen stops before the verdict, and none is a misfiled pre-verdict specimen that could be re-classified to populate the roster. The last real specimen, the logic_ground_truth trio, was deleted 2026-08-17 by #8283 (611fd02770); restoring its source verbatim produces `resolve failed: module v2.std.live_tree not found` — it dies on its own imports, which is a resolve error, not a pre-verdict refusal, and therefore cannot be enrolled either. The honest disposition is red-until-the-producer-exists, which is what this row carries.",

    "THE FIXTURE VARIANT, for when the red is in a SYNTHETIC claim rather than a real roster. A test module may hold a `data fixture_entry: String = \"some/path.dag\"` pointing at a file that has never existed in any revision, and drive claims over rows built from it (this very file's fixture_entry is such a pointer, and its four consumers at lines 138-155 and 285-291 are why it cannot simply be deleted). The claim then tests the PREDICATES against a made-up subject and can never fail for the reason it names, because no verifier ever walks the path. Where such a pointer has consumers, the repair is the missing fixture file, not the pointer; deleting the pointer to go green deletes the claim. For accepting the unresolvable-import case itself see gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unused_import_of_a_missing_module_is_accepted — that row is about a dangling IMPORT, which is now mechanically refused; a dangling STRING PATH used as data is a different harm and this is the row for it.",

    "NEIGHBOUR WHOSE CLASS THIS IS NOT. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_possibly_empty_producer_enters_a_nonempty_consumer is the nearest and it is NOT this: its INVALID STATE is a consumer that treats emptiness as impossible and stays SILENT when the population is empty. Here the consumer does the opposite — it refuses loudly and permanently — so nothing is silent and nothing is green. The shared shape is a population and a non-emptiness test; the difference is direction of the failure and, with it, the repair. That row's repair is to widen the consumer; this row's repair is to build the producer or to say the class is empty on purpose. Deleting the test is a repair that fits NEITHER and is the one most often chosen, because it is the only one that goes green today.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
